(* ============================================================================
   16. FOOTNOTES -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-025] (governs `footnote` / `footnote_definition`
      in PART 3). The reference form `[^label]` + `[^label]: body` and the
      INLINE form `^[content]` are implemented, and they are the only note
      forms: the proposed sidenote (`[>content]`) was dismissed as a
      presentation concern, so `[>` is unclaimed and `[>foo]` is literal
      text (docs/dismissed-syntax.md). Numbering and resolution are formalized
      as PART 9R rule R2; the rendering rules below stay normative here.
      - A `[^label]` reference with a matching definition is NUMBERED by
        document reference order (first referenced = 1); the same label
        referenced again reuses its number.
      - Definitions may appear anywhere (order-independent); the FIRST
        definition for a label wins. A body is the def line plus any
        following lines indented to column 2 or more (PART 9 §24 C1 column
        arithmetic -- a tab reaching column 2 qualifies the same as two
        spaces; single blank lines allowed between chunks), parsed as
        blocks. A definition is
        document-level metadata: it is COLLECTED and RESOLVED even when it
        sits INSIDE A CONTAINER. The reference resolves to the one endnotes
        section and the container that held the definition renders as
        though the line were never written -- §17 L6 states the general
        rule once, for every block-level container that can hold a
        definition, rather than enumerating them per definition kind. The
        corpus pins this (resources/examples/edge-cases.md "Footnote definition inside a
        container is collected").
      - A reference with NO matching definition renders as literal source
        text `[^label]` (a trailing `{attrs}` on an unresolved ref is not
        round-tripped -- a marginal mistyped-label edge). An UNREFERENCED
        definition is dropped.
      - Rendering (djot-compatible roles):
          reference -> <a id="{refId}" href="#fn{n}" role="doc-noteref"><sup>{n}</sup></a>
          endnotes  -> ONE <section role="doc-endnotes"> appended AFTER
                       all body content (outside heading <section>s),
                       containing <hr> then an <ol> of <li id="fn{n}">;
                       each note's last paragraph gets a trailing backlink
                       <a href="#{refId}" role="doc-backlink"
                          aria-label="{name}">↩</a>.
        First reference to note n has refId `fnref{n}`; a k-th (k>1)
        repeat uses `fnref{n}-{k}` and adds another backlink. The
        backlink glyph is the plain return arrow `↩` (Carve's choice;
        djot appends a variation selector).
        THE BACKLINK CARRIES AN ACCESSIBLE NAME -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-026]. The `role` alone
        was right and the NAME was the glyph, so a screen reader announced the
        character's Unicode name or skipped the link outright. `{name}` is the
        `footnoteBacklink` label (PART 9 §16a) followed by WHAT THE LINK VISIBLY
        SAYS -- the label alone for a lone backlink (`↩`, so
        `aria-label="Back to reference"`), the label plus k for the k-th of
        several (`↩<sup>k</sup>`, so `aria-label="Back to reference 2"`).
        Matching the visible text is WCAG 2.5.3, and it is why the number here
        is the REFERENCE ORDINAL and not the note number. The name is TEXT and
        is attribute-escaped.
        THE ENDNOTES SECTION CARRIES AN ACCESSIBLE NAME -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-027]. The rule
        above names the LINKS and left the REGION they sit in anonymous. The
        section takes `aria-label` when it has no authored title. The string is
        the `endnotes` label (SS16a), escaped the same way:
            <section role="doc-endnotes" aria-label="Footnotes">
        A placed `::: footnotes` with a quoted title instead takes
        `aria-labelledby` at that title's id under §12 [CARVE-P9-072].
        The default is "Footnotes" rather than "Endnotes" because that is the
        word the rest of this grammar uses for the construct. A host that
        renders them at the end and calls them endnotes sets the label; that is
        what the map is for.
      - A TOP-LEVEL `::: footnotes` THAT PLACES THE ENDNOTES SECTION -- NORMATIVE
        [CARVE-P9-075]. When at least one note is referenced, the first
        top-level `::: footnotes` places the one endnotes section at its
        position. Render each block authored in that directive's `children`
        in source order immediately BEFORE the section, as sibling blocks in
        the surrounding document or heading section. The authored blocks are
        not children of the generated endnotes section. The opener's quoted
        title and `[label]`, when present, instead become the section's first
        children under PART 9 §12 [CARVE-P9-072], before its `<hr>` and `<ol>`.
        Later `::: footnotes` markers, and a marker in a document with no
        referenced notes, render the ordinary `<div class="footnotes">` floor
        of §12 with their own title, label, and authored children. A nested
        marker follows [CARVE-P9-073] below, even when it appears before the
        first top-level marker.
      - DOCUMENT-WIDE PLACEMENT MARKERS REQUIRE DOCUMENT TOP LEVEL -- NORMATIVE
        [CARVE-P9-073]. A `::: footnotes`, `::: bibliography`, or
        `::: references` marker inside a block-level container -- a block
        quote, a list item, a div or directive body, a table cell, a definition
        description, a footnote definition -- does not place its document-wide
        region. It renders the `<div class="{kind}">` floor of §12 where it is
        written, and the region goes where it would without THIS marker. A
        processor with lint diagnostics reports `{kind}-placement-in-container`
        for the kind it supports. This rule does not restrict `::: toc`,
        `::: glossary`, or `::: index` inside a container.
      - A MARKER IN A TOC BODY RETAINS ITS CONTAINER SCOPE -- NORMATIVE
        [CARVE-P9-076]. With the `toc` placement extension enabled, a
        document-wide placement marker inside its body remains subject to
        CARVE-P9-073. Render its div fallback before the generated `<nav>`,
        where the extension emits its authored body. Moving that body ahead
        of the nav does not promote the nested marker to document top level.
        Its region goes where it would without that marker, including the
        default position when there is no other eligible placement marker.
      - BACKLINK PLACEMENT WHEN THE BODY DOES NOT END IN A PARAGRAPH. "The
        note's last paragraph" above presumes there is one. When the note
        body's LAST BLOCK is not a paragraph (a code block, a block quote, a
        table, a div, a raw block, or any other non-paragraph block), the
        backlink is never appended into a paragraph found INSIDE or BEFORE
        that block - the engine SYNTHESIZES a new wrapping <p> immediately
        AFTER the last block and places the backlink there, as a sibling of
        that block, never a descendant of it. This holds even when the last
        block itself ends in a paragraph of its own (a block quote whose
        content ends in a paragraph): that paragraph belongs to the quoted
        (or otherwise contained) content, not to the note's own backlink, so
        appending there would misattribute the backlink to the quotation. A
        note body consisting ONLY of a non-paragraph block still gets the
        synthesized trailing <p>. Appending to whatever paragraph happens to
        be last is undefined when the last block has no paragraph.
      - A BODY WHOSE EVERY BLOCK RENDERS NOTHING IS AN EMPTY BODY -- NORMATIVE
        [CARVE-P9-077]. The <li> holds the body's block elements, each on its
        own line per PART 10 §4, and then the synthesized backlink paragraph.
        Which lines those are is decided by what the blocks RENDER, not by how
        many blocks the body holds: a comment, a comment fence and a raw block
        the target does not match each produce no element, so a body holding
        only those renders as an empty body and its <li> opens directly on the
        backlink paragraph. A body holding a visible block ALONGSIDE an
        invisible one is not empty and spells the visible one on its own line.
        [CARVE-P10-001] already reads a CONTAINER body this way, and this clause
        says the endnote item reads its own the same way. What the two do with
        the answer differs, because an `<li>` always carries the backlink
        paragraph: an empty container body keeps a blank line, an empty note body
        keeps no line at all.
        An implementation that tells an empty body apart from an all-invisible
        one, and spells the second with a line the first does not get, is NOT
        CONFORMING: nothing downstream distinguishes the two documents.
      - INLINE FOOTNOTE `^[content]` (pandoc form; a deliberate carve
        extension -- canonical djot has no inline footnotes). A `^`
        IMMEDIATELY before `[` opens an anonymous note whose content is the
        balanced bracket span (escape- and code-span-aware close, same as link
        text). Properties:
          * Content is INLINE-only, parsed recursively with footnote
            recognition DISABLED inside it (no `^[…]` or `[^ref]` nested in a
            note, either direction).
          * Numbered in the SAME document-order sequence as reference notes
            (an inline note always takes a fresh anonymous number; it cannot
            be re-referenced). Renders the same noteref + an `<li id="fn{n}">`
            in the one endnotes section, content as one `<p>` + backlink.
          * A trailing `{attrs}` attaches to the noteref `<a>` (like a
            reference note).
          * Empty or whitespace-only (`^[]`, `^[ ]`) is literal; an unclosed
            `^[…` is literal.
        PRECEDENCE (PART 8): `^[` opens an inline note; a bare `^` anywhere
        else is literal text (there is no bare superscript). Consequences:
        `^[x]^` is a note then a literal `^`; `\^[x]` is literal; a `^` not
        immediately followed by `[` is plain text.
      - LIMITATION: a footnote (reference `[^1]` or inline `^[…]`) inside link
        text (`[t[^1]](u)`) or inside a heading later cloned by a `</#id>`
        crossref nests an <a> in an <a>; avoid footnotes in those positions.

   16a. ENGINE-WRITTEN LABELS are defined in the full PART 9 §16a clause below.

   17. TIGHT vs LOOSE LISTS + CONTINUATION MARKER -- OPERATIONAL (governs
      `list` rendering and the continuation_marker productions in PART 2).
      L1 LOOSE(list) := some item is followed by a blank line before the
         next sibling marker, OR some item holds a blank-line-separated
         second PARAGRAPH; otherwise TIGHT. This is a statement about ONE
         and TWO blank lines: a run of THREE OR MORE is SS11 N1a's hard
         boundary, so the marker below it is not a "next sibling marker" at
         all and there is no one list left to be loose. Tightness is a property of the
         WHOLE ITEM, not of an individual block: a tight item's paragraphs
         ALL render WITHOUT `<p>` (<li>text</li>), every one of them, not
         only the first; a loose item's paragraphs are ALL wrapped
         (<li><p>text</p></li>). An item can hold more than one paragraph
         block and still be tight -- L2 and L6 below cover the ways that
         happens without a blank line ever appearing.
      L1a THE ITEM'S FIRST BLOCK DOES NOT MATTER -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-029]. L1 asks
         whether the item holds a blank-line-separated second PARAGRAPH. It
         does not ask what the FIRST block was, and nothing here depends on
         that. So

             - - a

               Body.

         is LOOSE: the sub-list is the item's first block, `Body.` is a
         second paragraph after a blank line, and L1 is satisfied. The same
         holds for every other lead -- plain text, a heading, an attribute
         block, a block quote -- all of which core rendering handles
         loose.

      L1b AN INVISIBLE LINE DOES NOT CANCEL THE SEPARATION -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-030]. L1
         asks whether the item holds a blank-line-separated second PARAGRAPH.
         An INVISIBLE LINE (§10 I5, AN INVISIBLE LINE IS ONE CLASSIFICATION)
         emits no visible block, which is why it cannot BE the second one
         (L1a's argument), and by the same clause's property 3 it
         does not choose the next line's owner either: it is not a separator,
         so the separation it appears to interrupt is intact and the item is
         LOOSE. This clause is that property's consequence for list
         TIGHTNESS.

             - a

               %% n
               text

         is loose. `text` is a second paragraph, the blank line precedes it,
         and the comment renders nothing.

      L1c THE BLANK IS READ AT THE LIST'S LEVEL, NOT THE ITEM'S. L1's first
         disjunct asks what stands BETWEEN one item and the next sibling
         marker. A blank line with nothing of the item after it stands between
         them however the item's interior accounted for the line -- including
         where an unterminated container carries it as content and the output
         shows it doing so, as an unterminated code fence's empty payload line
         does. So

             - ```           - ::: d
               b               b

             - s             - s

         are both LOOSE, and so are the same documents with the closer
         written. The state of the container above the blank does not enter
         this question either, which is the same property PART 1 S4 already
         states for where the item ENDS.

         An INTERIOR fence blank does not loosen the list: content follows it
         before the sibling marker, so no blank line separates the items --

             - ```
               a

               b
               ```
             - c

      L2 COMPACT LIST BLOCKS -- Carve deviation from djot/CommonMark. A
         blank line before an item's sub-BLOCK (sub-list, block quote,
         fenced code, fenced div, heading, table) does NOT loosen: the item
         stays tight, lead text inline, block attached. Only a genuine
         second PARAGRAPH (blank + indented plain prose), or a blank line
         BETWEEN items, loosens. The blank line is still required to START
         the block, so recognition and the uniformity principle are
         unchanged -- only the tight/loose RENDERING differs (djot renders
         these loose). Pinned by corpus compact-list-blocks.
      L3 CONTINUATION MARKER `+` -- Carve addition. A line whose only
         content is `+`, at the current container's MARKER COLUMN, attaches
         the FOLLOWING flush-left block to that container -- ONE block of
         ANY kind (paragraph, list, fenced code, table, block quote, div,
         ...), up to the next blank line, sibling marker, or a further `+`
         -- with no marker prefix or indentation, keeping the container
         tight. `+` is not a Carve bullet (§11 N1), so a lone `+` is
         unambiguous; outside any container a lone `+` is literal text.
         The marker only ATTACHES, never separates: a blank line is the
         sole way to END a container, so a `+` can never break the
         following block OUT of its container -- it adds it IN.

         CONTINUATION-MARKER FLUSH-LEFT MEANS COLUMN 0 -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-031].
         "flush-left" above is the reach, not a description of the usual case:
         the marker attaches a block that begins at column 0 and NOTHING else.
         A line at any other column is not attached at all - it falls through
         to the ordinary column rules, which place it by its own column inside
         whichever container survives. A `+` at a column no container's marker
         column names was never this marker: like the lone `+` outside any
         container above, it is ordinary text and reaches the output.

         The single-level spelling is unaffected in practice, which is why this
         went unnoticed: under `- +` a line at column 2 is the item's OWN
         content column, so the ordinary rules put it in the item whether the
         marker reached it or not.

         ONE IS A COUNT AND THE BOUNDARY IS NOT -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-032].
         The blank line, sibling marker or further `+` above is the EXTENT of
         that single block, which may be many lines long -- a list, a quote,
         a fenced block. It is not a run boundary, and the marker does not
         attach everything up to it. So

             - a
             +
             para
             > q

         attaches `para` and leaves the quote OUTSIDE the item, and a second
         attached block takes a second marker:

             - a
             +
             para
             +
             > q

         THE MARKER IS ONE OPERATION IN EVERY CONTAINER -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-033].
         The clause above is the WHOLE definition of `+`:
         ownership of the next flush-left block passes to the container, and
         that block is then parsed like any other. A container may not answer
         differently because of WHAT it is attaching, and there is no second
         rule anywhere for a quote, a list, a fence, a caption or a definition
         under the marker. In particular the ONE BLOCK count and the boundary
         set above hold in EVERY container in L4, not only in the two that had
         them spelled out.

         At the host's marker column, the marker is outside the content column
         of a preceding code or raw fence. That fence ends before the attached
         block begins. Separators inserted for attachment are not fence payload.
         With no following block, the marker adds no content or blank line.
         A `+` inside the fence's own content column remains literal payload.

      L4 CONTAINERS THAT TAKE THE MARKER:
         - LIST ITEM (pinned by corpus list-continuation-marker): `+` at
           the item's marker column attaches the block to the item (for
           code/tables one would rather not indent). FIRST-BLOCK form: the
           marker may sit on the marker line itself (`- +`, a bare `+`) to
           open an item whose body is the following flush-left BLOCK, one
           of them as everywhere else in L3, with no inline lead;
           `- + text` keeps `+ text` as literal text.
         - BLOCK QUOTE: `+` at column 0 immediately after a blockquote line
           attaches the following flush-left block to the quote -- the
           un-prefixed alternative to repeating `>`. "> quoted \n + \n
           - item" is a quote whose body is the paragraph "quoted" PLUS a
           real <ul> (not a lazily-folded paragraph, cf. §10 I6). A blank
           line after the quote still ends it. An attached block that is
           ITSELF a quote NESTS, like every other kind: `> a` / `+` / `> q`
           is a quote holding a paragraph and an inner quote. The marker
           attaching nothing there, and the line continuing the outer quote
           instead, was the kind test named in L3.
         - FOOTNOTE DEFINITION body: `+` at column 0 attaches the following
           flush-left block to the note, the alternative to indenting it to
           the body's column (pinned by corpus
           footnote-with-multiple-blocks).
         - DEFINITION DESCRIPTION: the same marker, the same reach, for a
           `<dd>` (`definition_body` carries `continuation_marker_block`).

         THE FIRST-BLOCK FORM IS THE ITEM AND THE DESCRIPTION -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-067].
         The four containers above take the marker; only TWO of
         them take it in the FIRST-BLOCK position. `- +` and `: +` open a body
         whose content is the following flush-left block, and over a line that
         is not flush-left the marker is refused and the body ends. A FOOTNOTE
         BODY and a BLOCK QUOTE have no such form: `[^a]: +` and `> +` open a
         body whose first line is a lone `+`, and there the `+` is NOT A MARKER
         AT ALL. It is ordinary text, the payload lands wherever the ordinary
         column rules put it, and no column is read as a marker column --

             [^a]: +
             flush

         is a note whose text is `+` with `flush` a top-level paragraph, and

             > +
             flush

         is a quote holding one paragraph `+ flush`, the second line reaching
         the first by §10 I6's lazy fold. At the body's own column the payload
         folds into the paragraph the `+` opened, so `[^a]: +` over a column-2
         line is the single paragraph `+ flush` inside the note.

         That is a DIFFERENT ANSWER from "place the payload by its column", not
         a narrower one, which is why it is stated rather than left to the
         reader of L3. Extending the first-block form to the other two
         containers was the alternative, and it is not what this clause does.
      L5 NOT CONTINUATION CONTAINERS (a lone `+` keeps its plain meaning):
         - fenced containers (admonition / generic div / line block) --
           their bodies already hold flush-left blocks; a lone `+` inside
           is literal text;
         - TABLE -- a first-column `+` is the continuation ROW (§5 T6), a
           distinct construct;
         - LEAF blocks (heading, thematic break, code block, raw block,
           paragraph) hold no child blocks; a heading is a bounded title.
      L6 A COLLECTED DEFINITION LEAVES NO TRACE -- NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-034] (the general
         rule for PART 9R R1/R2 COLLECTION, stated ONCE here for every
         container that can hold a definition). This is §10 I5's property 2 --
         emits no visible block at its authored location -- for the two
         definitions that also REGISTER, which is their semantic action; the
         property itself is stated at AN INVISIBLE LINE IS ONE CLASSIFICATION
         and is not re-derived here. A link reference or
         footnote definition is document-level metadata: wherever it is
         written, PASS 1 collects it into the shared symbol table and PASS
         2 resolves uses against it from anywhere in the document (PART
         9R). The BLOCK-LEVEL CONTEXT that held the definition line renders
         as though that line were never written at all -- not an empty
         paragraph standing in its place, not a blank line, not residual
         whitespace. This holds for EVERY block-level context that can hold
         block content: a LIST ITEM, a BLOCK QUOTE, a FOOTNOTE BODY (§16),
         a DEFINITION-LIST `dd`, and a block attached by a `+` CONTINUATION
         MARKER (L3/L4) alike. A future container that can hold block content
         is covered by the same rule.

         "No trace" is about OUTPUT, not about block structure -- it does
         not exempt a definition line from §10 I5, which already governs
         paragraph interruption everywhere, containers included (I6): a
         definition line INTERRUPTS an open paragraph exactly as any other
         invisible construct does, ending it with no blank line needed, so
         a plain line immediately after a definition starts a NEW block
         rather than folding back into the paragraph the definition
         interrupted. What decides how that new block RENDERS is L1/L2, not
         L6: no blank line appeared anywhere, so the item stays TIGHT
         (L2 loosens on a blank line, never on block count alone), and by
         L1 a tight item's paragraphs are ALL bare, this new one included --
         not because the definition is transparent to structure, but
         because nothing here ever loosened the item in the first place.
         A footnote definition sitting on a list item's OWN line, followed
         by a plain continuation line, is this exact shape: the item holds
         TWO paragraph blocks with no blank between them and renders both
         bare, `<li>first\n  second\n</li>`. Treating the definition as if it
         both interrupted AND merged would need a rule found nowhere else in
         this grammar; treating it as loosening the item on its own would
         contradict L2 directly.

      L7 ONE CONSUMED BOOLEAN SPELLS THE LOOSENESS NO BLANK LINE CAN --
         NORMATIVE [CARVE-P9-035]. A container's preceding BLOCK-ATTRIBUTE LINE
         (§15) MAY carry the boolean `loose`. It says that the container's
         children render as BLOCKS rather than as inline runs, and it is
         CONSUMED: it never reaches the output as an HTML attribute. The
         precedent is PART 12 §15's `header-rows`, which rides the same line,
         carries a structural fact as a boolean, and is likewise consumed rather
         than emitted.

             {loose}
             - Note text.

         renders

             <ul>
               <li><p>Note text.</p></li>
             </ul>

         THE KEY IS NOT LIST-SPECIFIC, and writing it as if it were would have
         been the first of at least two one-off keys. The tight/loose axis
         exists in exactly TWO places, and this key applies wherever it exists:
         a LIST (L1) and a DEFINITION LIST (PART 2, definition lists), and
         any container a later clause gives the axis to, with no second key to
         invent. A table cell and a caption suppress the paragraph wrapper with
         no wrapped alternative at all; a block quote, div, line block and
         footnote body always wrap. On a container with no such axis the name
         has no special meaning and renders `loose=""` like any other boolean.

         WHAT IS UNSPELLABLE WITHOUT IT. Looseness is spelled with a blank line,
         and a blank line needs two things to stand between, so the shape with
         nothing on one side has no spelling:

           - a ONE-ITEM list. `<ul><li><p>x</p></li></ul>` has no "between
             items" to put a blank line in.
           - a definition description holding ONE block. A blank line between
             two ENTRIES does not loosen a `<dl>` at all -- only a second block
             inside the description wraps it -- so `<dd><p>x</p></dd>` is
             unspellable at every entry count, not just at one.


         THERE IS NO `tight` TWIN, and its absence is a decision rather than an
         omission. TIGHT IS ALWAYS SPELLABLE: remove the blank lines. Only loose
         has an unspellable case, so a `tight` key would be surface with no
         shape behind it -- and it would introduce a genuinely ambiguous
         document, where the blank lines say loose and the attribute says tight.

         THE NAME IS THE TERM OF ART, on purpose. A name describing the tree
         would name the wrong fact -- the children are already block nodes in
         both spellings, and what moves is how they RENDER -- and a name
         describing the output would be an HTML artifact, where this axis has to
         mean something on the Markdown, plain and terminal targets too. `loose`
         is what CommonMark, djot and Markdown Extra all call this distinction.

         REDUNDANT USE IS A NO-OP. `loose` on a list the blank lines already
         made loose is legal and changes nothing, and so is `loose` on a
         description that already holds two blocks. Rejecting it would make the
         key context-sensitive, and a producer that always emits it is simpler
         than one that has to decide.

         THE CANONICAL WRITER EMITS IT ONLY WHERE THE BLANK-LINE SPELLING
         CANNOT. This follows PART 12 §15, whose writer "retains `header-rows` /
         `footer-rows` when they are present" rather than deriving them onto
         every table, and PART 11 §2, which spends a mark only where omitting it
         would change the re-parsed document. On a multi-item loose list the
         blank lines already say it, so the key would be an idle mark.

         THE TEST IS A RE-PARSE, AND IT IS OVER THE DOCUMENT. Write the
         container's body without the key, read it back, and emit the key
         exactly where the container's own looseness field did not survive that
         trip. The equality is PART 11 §1's, taken over the re-parsed document;
         taking it over the RENDER instead answers a different question.

         THE ITEM COUNT IS NOT THE TEST. A ONE-ITEM list is loose whenever the
         blank line sits INSIDE that item, and there the blank-line spelling did
         express the looseness, so a writer keyed on the item count rewrites
         such a document and fails `parse(fmt(x)) == parse(x)`. The count is
         sound as a SHORTCUT IN ONE DIRECTION ONLY: two or more items always
         re-parse loose, so the read may be skipped there.

         THE COUNT IS ALSO WRONG IN THE OTHER DIRECTION. A definition list's
         entries count as two or more on any list worth writing, and a blank
         line between two ENTRIES does not loosen a `<dl>` at any count - so a
         writer keyed on the count OMITS the key from the container that needs
         it most.

         ON A DEFINITION LIST THE ANSWER IS UNCONDITIONAL: the writer emits the
         key on every definition list carrying `loose`. PART 12 §8 publishes
         `definition_list.loose` ONLY where the looseness was SPELLED, so a body
         written without the key can never read back with the field set, and the
         re-parse test says "emit" every time. A description that already holds
         two blocks does not change it: there the key is redundant in the RENDER
         and not in the TREE, and the tree is what the equality is taken over.

         ORDERED AND NESTED CONTAINERS TAKE THE SAME KEY AT THE SAME PLACEMENT,
         at the nested container's own indent, so this needs no rediscovering:
         `{loose}` before `1.` loosens an `<ol>`, and an attribute line written
         at a sub-list's indentation loosens the sub-list and not its parent.

         A BOOLEAN AND AN EMPTY VALUE ARE ONE KEY; A VALUED ONE IS NOT. PART 4
         makes `{loose}` and `{loose=""}` the same attribute, so both are
         consumed. `loose=x` names a value this key does not take, so it is not
         this key: it stays an ordinary attribute and renders `loose="x"`. There
         is no error state to define, and no spelling that half-applies.

         EACH CONTAINER CARRIES THE KEY'S EFFECT IN ITS OWN FIELD, and PART 12
         §6's round trip covers both. A loosened LIST sets `list.tight` false --
         the field is required there, and the axis is total, so nothing else is
         needed. A loosened DEFINITION LIST sets `definition_list.loose`, which
         PART 12 §8 defines: the `<dd>` wrapper is otherwise derived from the
         description's block count, and that derivation cannot reach the one
         shape this key exists for, so the SPELLED fact is published and nothing
         else is. The two fields are named differently because they
         say different things: `tight` states the whole axis for a list, while
         `loose` records only what a definition list's own derivation misses.


   ============================================================================ *)
